<|im_start|>user
Problem: During the World Cup, there are \( n \) different Panini stickers to collect. Marco's friends are trying to complete their collection, but nobody has a full set of stickers yet! A pair of his friends are said to be wholesome if their combined collection has at least one of each sticker. Marco knows the contents of everyone's collections, and wants to take them all to a restaurant for his birthday. However, he doesn't want any wholesome pairs sitting at the same table.

(i) Show that Marco might need to reserve at least \( n \) different tables.

(ii) Show that \( n \) tables will always be enough for Marco to achieve his goal.

Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.<|im_end|>
<|im_start|>assistant


Completion:
We are given a problem involving **Panini stickers**, where there are $ n $ different stickers to collect. Marco's friends each have some subset of these stickers, and a **pair of friends** is called **wholesome** if **their combined collection contains at least one of each sticker** (i.e., every sticker from 1 to $ n $ appears in at least one of the two collections).

Marco wants to **seat all his friends** at tables such that **no wholesome pair** sits at the **same table**.

We are to:

**(i)** Show that Marco **might need to reserve at least $ n $** different tables.

**(ii)** Show that **$ n $ tables will always be enough**.

We'll solve this step-by-step.

---

### Step 1: Understand the Definitions

Each person has a subset $ S_i \subseteq \{1, 2, \ldots, n\} $, the collection of stickers they have.

Two people $ i $ and $ j $ form a **wholesome pair** if:

$$
S_i \cup S_j = \{1, 2, \ldots, n\}
$$

In other words, together they own **all** $ n $ stickers.

We want to **partition** the set of friends into groups (tables) such that **no two people at the same table** form a wholesome pair.

Our task is to show:

- (i) **At least $ n $ tables** may be **necessary** in some cases.
- (ii) **Always $ n $ tables** are **sufficient**.

That is, the **minimum number** of tables required to avoid wholesome pairs among friends in the same group may be as high as $ n $, and **always** $ n $ suffice.

This seems to be related to **extremal set theory** and **combinatorics**, particularly with **union coverings** and **separating systems**.

---

### Step 2: Interpret the Goal as a Coloring or Partition Problem

Let $ F $ be the set of friends, each with a sticker collection $ S_i \subseteq [n] $.

We want to partition $ F $ into disjoint groups (tables), such that **no pair $ (i,j) $** with $ S_i \cup S_j = [n] $ sits in the **same group**.

That is, **within** a group, for any pair $ (i,j) $, $ S_i \cup S_j \ne [n] $.

Alternatively: **For each table**, the collection of sticker sets $ \{S_i \mid i \text{ at table}\} $ satisfies that **no two sets** $ S_i, S_j $ cover $ [n] $.

So we are avoiding the appearance of **a pair** whose union is $ [n] $.

This is a **graph-theoretic** problem:

- Make a **graph** $ G $ where:
  - Each vertex = a friend
  - Edge between $ i $ and $ j $ iff $ S_i \cup S_j = [n] $

This is a **"wholesome pair" graph**.

We are to **partition the vertices** of this graph into **independent sets** (i.e., no edge inside a set).

Thus, the **chromatic number** $ \chi(G) $ of this graph is the **minimum number of tables** required.

We are to show:

- (i) There **exists** a configuration of sticker collections (i.e., sets $ S_i $) such that $ \chi(G) \ge n $.

- (ii) For **any** such configuration, $ \chi(G) \le n $.

We are not told the number of friends, so this will hold for arbitrary collections.

That is, in **every possible scenario**, $ n $ tables are **enough**.

And in **some scenario**, **$ n $ tables are necessary**.

---

### Step 3: Part (i) — Show $ n $ tables may be *necessary*

We need to **construct** a collection of sticker sets $ S_1, S_2, \ldots, S_m $ (where $ m $ is number of friends) such that:

- For each $ i = 1, 2, \ldots, n $, there is **a person** who is **missing** sticker $ i $.

- The **pair** of two people with collections $ S_i, S_j $ will cover $ [n] $ **if and only if** $ S_i \cup S_j = [n] $.

And we need the **graph** of wholesome pairs (i.e., edges when union is $ [n] $) to **require** $ n $ colors (tables).

We will **construct** a collection of $ n $ friends with **very structured** sticker sets,
